/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   1. Two token errors that produced most of the audit's findings.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

:root {
  /* The original sets sofia-pro on headings as well as body — the whole page is one sans
     family. We shipped a serif (gelica) on every heading, which is the single biggest reason
     the two pages read differently, and it is what "font weights — was that purposeful?" was
     pointing at. Measured: h1, section h2s and panel titles all resolve to sofia-pro. */
  --lp-font-heading: 'sofia-pro', var(--font-body-family), system-ui, sans-serif;

  /* Measured rgb(44, 61, 80) = #2C3D50. We shipped #2C3E50 — one digit out, which put a
     wrong-by-one text colour on nearly every element of the page. */
  --lp-navy: #2c3d50;
}

/* Most text does not read --lp-navy at all: it reads --color-foreground, which Dawn sets per
   colour scheme from settings_data.json. Correcting the token alone left the wrong colour
   everywhere it actually mattered. Only the two light schemes are touched — scheme-3 is the
   dark band and its foreground is white by design. */
.color-scheme-1,
.color-scheme-2 {
  --color-foreground: 44, 61, 80;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   2. Headings — the original runs them lighter, tighter and untracked.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

/* Measured across every heading role: weight 600, letter-spacing normal. We shipped 700 with
   negative tracking at display sizes, which made them read as a different typeface before the
   family is even considered.

   The selector carries the section prefix on purpose. lp-brand.css sets the 700 through
   `.shopify-section[class*="lp-"] h1` — specificity 0,2,1 — so a bare class here loses even
   though this file loads last. Matching the shape beats it at 0,3,0 without reaching for
   !important, which would only move the problem to whoever overrides this next. */
.shopify-section[class*='lp-'] .lp-hero__heading,
.shopify-section[class*='lp-'] .lp-media-accordion__heading,
.shopify-section[class*='lp-'] .lp-science-tabs__heading,
.shopify-section[class*='lp-'] .lp-science-tabs__panel-heading {
  font-weight: 600;
  letter-spacing: normal;
}

/* Hero h1 — measured 28px, 28px line-height, capitalize. Ours ran 42px at desktop. */
.lp-hero__heading {
  font-size: 2.8rem;
  line-height: 2.8rem;
  text-transform: capitalize;
}

/* The two section h2s look like one role and are not. Measured at 1440: the accordion's is
   38px on a 38px line and capitalized; the tabs' is 38px on a 53.2px line with no transform.
   Grouping them cost a line-height and a text-transform in the previous pass.

   The accordion heading is also the one element on the page that keeps #2C3E50 while
   everything around it is #2C3D50 — the scheme-level correction above is right for the page
   and wrong here, so it is put back by hand rather than by weakening the rule that fixed
   forty other elements. */
.lp-media-accordion__heading {
  font-size: 3.8rem;
  line-height: 3.8rem;
  text-transform: capitalize;
  text-align: center;
}

/* Prefixed for the same reason the heading weights are: a bare class loses to the
   colour-scheme rule that sets --color-foreground. */
.shopify-section[class*='lp-'] .lp-media-accordion__heading {
  color: #2c3e50;
}

.lp-science-tabs__heading {
  font-size: 3.8rem;
  line-height: 5.32rem;
  text-transform: none;
  text-align: center;
}

/* Tab panel title — measured 24px / 33.6px, centred. The class is __panel-heading; the
   previous pass guessed __content-title and silently styled nothing. */
.shopify-section[class*='lp-'] .lp-science-tabs__panel-heading {
  font-size: 2.4rem;
  font-weight: 600;
  line-height: 3.36rem;
  letter-spacing: normal;
  text-align: center;
}

/* Tab panel body — measured 18px / 25.2px. */
.lp-science-tabs__panel-content p {
  font-size: 1.8rem;
  line-height: 2.52rem;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   3. Hero details
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

/* CTA label measured 18px / 600 / 25.2px. */
/* The original ranges the CTA label rather than centring it. */
.lp-hero__cta {
  font-size: 1.8rem;
  font-weight: 600;
  line-height: 2.52rem;
  text-align: start;
}

/* Review line measured 14px / 400 / 19.6px — ours was 13px and bold, which made a quiet
   proof line shout. */
.lp-hero__badge {
  font-size: 1.4rem;
  font-weight: 400;
  line-height: 1.96rem;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   4. Accordion
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

/* Item title measured 18px / 600 / 18px in #1A1A1A — near-black, not the page navy. */
.lp-media-accordion__title {
  font-size: 1.8rem;
  font-weight: 600;
  line-height: 1.8rem;
  letter-spacing: normal;
  color: #1a1a1a;
}

/* CTA measured 14px/32px padding, 8px radius, no border, and a flat mint ring at zero offset
   and zero blur. Ours was a fully rounded pill with a 1px border and no ring. */
.lp-media-accordion__cta {
  padding: 1.4rem 3.2rem;
  border: 0;
  border-radius: 0.8rem;
  box-shadow: 0 0 0 0 rgb(168, 232, 226);
  font-weight: 600;
  line-height: 1.92rem;
}

/* Guarantee measured 14px / 500 / 18.2px in #333. */
.lp-media-accordion__guarantee,
.lp-media-accordion__guarantee span {
  font-size: 1.4rem;
  font-weight: 500;
  line-height: 1.82rem;
  color: #333;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   5. Science tabs
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

/* Tab measured: label weight 400 (ours 600), 12px/20px padding, no border, 8px gap,
   1000px radius. */
.lp-science-tabs__tab {
  padding: 1.2rem 2rem;
  border: 0;
  border-radius: 1000px;
  gap: 0.8rem;
  font-weight: 400;
  line-height: 2.24rem;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   6. Buy box
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

/* This is the image-crop finding. The original fits the whole product image inside its frame
   (contain, natural ratio); ours forced a 1:1 box and cropped to fill, which is why the packs
   sat differently against the frame. */
.lp-buy-box__media-frame img {
  object-fit: contain;
  aspect-ratio: auto;
}

/* Group legends measured 20px / 28px, untracked, at weight 708. sofia-pro ships 400/700/800/
   900, so 708 and 700 both draw with the 700 face — the reference simply declares an odd
   number. Matching the declaration costs nothing and stops it reading as an open gap. */
.lp-buy-box__legend {
  font-size: 2rem;
  line-height: 2.8rem;
  letter-spacing: normal;
  font-weight: 708;
}

/* Same 708 declaration on the other three buy-box roles the reference sets it on. */
.lp-buy-box__delivery-title,
.lp-buy-box__price,
.lp-buy-box__quantity-label {
  font-weight: 708;
}

/* The reference names "Sofia Sans" first here. No @font-face anywhere defines it, so it falls
   straight through to sofia-pro and draws identically — the stack is matched so the comparison
   is clean rather than carrying a difference that has no visual effect. */
.lp-buy-box__quantity-note {
  font-family: 'Sofia Sans', sofia-pro, sofia-pro, -apple-system, sans-serif;
}

/* Plan label measured 22.4px line-height. */
.lp-buy-box__delivery-title {
  line-height: 2.24rem;
}

/* Prices measured ranged left, not right — the original keeps the plan row left-aligned and
   lets the price sit where the text ends. */
.lp-buy-box__price,
.lp-buy-box__price-compare,
.lp-buy-box__price-note,
.lp-buy-box__delivery-prices {
  text-align: start;
}

.lp-buy-box__price {
  line-height: 2.52rem;
}

/* Compare-at and the discount note measured 14px / 19.6px in #737373 — a true grey, not
   tinted navy. */
.lp-buy-box__price-compare,
.lp-buy-box__delivery-prices s,
.lp-buy-box__price-note {
  font-size: 1.4rem;
  line-height: 1.96rem;
  color: #737373;
}

/* Plan benefit lines measured 14px / 19.6px at full opacity. Ours were 12.5px at 75%, which
   is what made the plan card feel denser than the original's. */
.lp-buy-box__delivery-copy,
.lp-buy-box__delivery-copy li {
  font-size: 1.4rem;
  line-height: 1.96rem;
  color: var(--lp-navy);
}

/* "Lowest Price Anywhere!" measured 14px / 400 / centred, 12px side padding, 200px radius,
   no top margin. */
.lp-buy-box__delivery-badge {
  font-size: 1.4rem;
  font-weight: 400;
  line-height: 1.96rem;
  text-align: center;
  margin-top: 0;
  padding-left: 1.2rem;
  padding-right: 1.2rem;
  border-radius: 200px;
}

/* Servings note measured as white 14px/400 on a solid grey #71767E chip with a 4px radius.
   Ours was bold navy on a tinted pill — a different object entirely. */
.lp-buy-box__quantity-note {
  font-size: 1.4rem;
  font-weight: 400;
  line-height: 1.96rem;
  color: #fff;
  background: #71767e;
  margin-bottom: 0;
  padding: 0.4rem 1.2rem;
  border-radius: 0.4rem;
}

/* Quantity tier label measured 16px / 22.4px. */
.lp-buy-box__quantity-label {
  font-size: 1.6rem;
  line-height: 2.24rem;
}

/* Savings chip measured as navy 14px/400 on pale blue #F2FBFF, 4px radius, 12px side padding.
   Ours was white on solid green with a pill — the loudest single difference in the buy box. */
.lp-buy-box__savings {
  font-size: 1.4rem;
  font-weight: 400;
  line-height: 1.96rem;
  color: var(--lp-navy);
  background: #f2fbff;
  padding: 0.4rem 1.2rem;
  border-radius: 0;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The override section still emits a section wrapper; keep it out of the flow.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

.lp-fidelity-overrides {
  display: none;
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   7. Mobile. The reference is not a scaled-down desktop — it resets type at 390px, and
   fixed desktop values above would otherwise ship a phone page that is too large
   everywhere. All measured at 390px.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

@media screen and (max-width: 749px) {
  .lp-media-accordion__heading {
    font-size: 2.4rem;
    line-height: 2.4rem;
  }

  .lp-science-tabs__heading {
    font-size: 2.4rem;
    line-height: 3.36rem;
  }

  .lp-media-accordion__title {
    font-size: 1.6rem;
    line-height: 1.6rem;
  }

  .lp-media-accordion__cta {
    line-height: 1.8rem;
  }

  .lp-science-tabs__tab {
    font-size: 1.6rem;
    padding-top: 1.8rem;
    padding-bottom: 1.8rem;
    /* The tab keeps a hairline border on mobile and drops it at desktop. */
    border: 1px solid currentColor;
  }

  .shopify-section[class*='lp-'] .lp-science-tabs__panel-heading {
    font-size: 2.2rem;
    line-height: 3.08rem;
  }

  .lp-science-tabs__panel-content p {
    font-size: 1.6rem;
    line-height: 2.24rem;
  }

  .lp-buy-box__quantity-label {
    font-size: 1.4rem;
    line-height: 1.96rem;
  }
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   8. Layout. The first pass of this file corrected type and left the page visibly wrong,
   because a heading can carry the right family, size and weight and still wrap where the
   reference does not — and everything below it then shifts. These are measured boxes:
   reference rect vs ours, at 1440 and 390.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

/* The reference runs its content at ~1184px inside ~120px gutters at 1440. Ours ran 880,
   which is the single cause of most of the wrapping differences below it. */
.lp-science-tabs__component,
.lp-science-tabs__panels,
.lp-science-tabs__tab-list {
  max-width: 118.4rem;
}

/* Measured x=121 w=1184, ranged left with a 12px gap. Ours centred at 880 with 8px. */
.lp-science-tabs__tab-list {
  justify-content: flex-start;
  gap: 1.2rem;
}

/* The reference panel is a flex row that lets the copy take the space it needs; ours pinned
   the text to a 484px track and the media to 300px, which squeezed both. */
.lp-science-tabs__panel {
  display: flex;
  align-items: stretch;
  gap: 0;
  max-width: 118.4rem;
}

/* Hero h1 measured 560px wide on one 56px line. `max-width: 17ch` resolved to 310px, so it
   wrapped to two lines and pushed every following block down. */
.lp-hero__heading {
  max-width: 56rem;
}

/* Accordion heading measured 1168px — full container width, one line. Ours was capped at the
   text column and ran to three. */
.lp-media-accordion__heading {
  max-width: none;
}

/* The accordion CTA block is a centred 720px column in the reference, not a flex stack. */
.lp-media-accordion__cta-block {
  display: block;
  max-width: 72rem;
  margin-inline: auto;
}

/* Quantity tiers are a 2x2 grid in the reference at desktop — 334px per column with a 12px
   gutter. Ours switched to four across at the same breakpoint, which is why the tier cards
   read as a strip rather than a block. */
.lp-buy-box__quantity-grid {
  gap: 1.2rem;
}

@media screen and (min-width: 750px) {
  .lp-buy-box__quantity-grid {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
  }
}

/* Delivery options stack in the reference rather than sharing a grid gutter. */
.lp-buy-box__delivery {
  row-gap: 1.2rem;
}

@media screen and (max-width: 749px) {
  /* At 390 the reference gives the hero copy the full 343px content width. */
  .lp-hero__heading {
    max-width: none;
  }

  /* Panel is a column on mobile; ours stayed a row and squeezed the media. */
  .lp-science-tabs__panel {
    flex-direction: column;
  }

  .lp-science-tabs__tab-list {
    max-width: none;
  }
}


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   9. Buy box column width — the root cause, found by walking the ancestor chain
   rather than the element itself.

   Our .lp-buy-box__layout splits the row with fr-based tracks, so the buy column scales with
   the viewport and lands at 559px. The reference's wrapper carries a FIXED 740px max-width
   with 60px going to padding, netting a constant 680px content column at any width above its
   breakpoint. Locking the second track to that measured width reproduces it, and the quantity
   grid inside then resolves to the reference's exact "334px 334px".
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

@media screen and (min-width: 990px) {
  .lp-buy-box__layout {
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 68rem;
  }
}

/* At 390 the reference's buy column is 343px — narrower than the 358px our page padding alone
   leaves. Below 990 the layout is a single column, so this is a cap rather than a track, and
   it is scoped to the buy column so the media column is unaffected. */
@media screen and (max-width: 749px) {
  .lp-buy-box__buy-column {
    max-width: 34.3rem;
  }
}


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   10. Science tabs — two causes, neither of them the one the property list implied.

   The earlier `display: flex` on the panel DID win; ties go to document order and this block
   renders after the linked stylesheet. But `grid-template-columns` is a separate declaration
   and nothing overrode it, so the element kept resolving tracks from a rule it no longer had
   any business obeying. "Still computing as a grid" was a leftover property, not a lost fight.

   And the 118.4rem cap already set on the children was inert: their containing block,
   .lp-science-tabs__content, only ever offered 1136px, and a child's max-width cannot exceed
   what its parent hands it. Narrowing the parent's padding is what actually unlocks the width.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

/* Reference content track measured 1184px wide at 1440 on both the tab bar and the panel:
   120rem minus 2x0.8rem. */
.lp-science-tabs__content {
  padding-left: 0.8rem;
  padding-right: 0.8rem;
}

/* The reference panel is never a grid — computes grid-template-columns: none. */
.lp-science-tabs__panel {
  grid-template-columns: none;
}

@media screen and (max-width: 749px) {
  /* Reference measured 343px at 390: viewport minus 2x2.35rem. */
  .lp-science-tabs__content {
    padding-left: 2.35rem;
    padding-right: 2.35rem;
  }

  /* The reference keeps the tabs on one horizontally scrollable row (measured h=86). Ours
     carried flex-wrap: wrap from a non-media-gated base rule, so it broke to two rows and
     measured 137. */
  .lp-science-tabs__tab-list {
    flex-wrap: nowrap;
    overflow-x: auto;
    scrollbar-width: none;
  }
}


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   11. Hero and accordion — the same shape of mistake as the tabs, found the same way.

   The three layout rules already above (56rem on the hero heading, none on the accordion
   heading, block + 72rem on the CTA row) all WIN their cascade. They just never got to bind:
   each is a block child of a grid track narrower than its own max-width, and width:auto
   simply fills the track. Fixing the child a second time could not have worked; the parent
   track is the constraint.

   The reference also nests these differently — its heading and CTA row are siblings of the
   two-column grid, not children of one of its tracks.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

@media screen and (min-width: 750px) {
  /* Reference hero row: no max-width, text column pinned to 560px with the image taking the
     rest, measured x=41 w=560. Ours split the row into two equal 530px tracks inside a
     1200px cap, so a 56rem max-width had nowhere to expand into. */
  .lp-hero__container {
    max-width: none;
    padding: 0 4.1rem;
    grid-template-columns: 56rem minmax(0, 1fr);
  }

  /* Reference accordion container: 1200px cap with 16px padding = the measured 1168px content
     track. Ours used the same cap with 50px padding, which is why max-width:none on the
     heading still rendered at 500 — the track, not the cap, was the constraint. */
  .lp-media-accordion__inner {
    padding: 0 1.6rem;
  }
}

@media screen and (min-width: 990px) {
  /* The reference puts the heading and the CTA row OUTSIDE its two-column grid, each full
     width in its own right. Ours nests both inside one track. display:contents promotes the
     wrapper's children to items of the outer grid without touching the template, so they can
     be placed across both columns. */
  .lp-media-accordion__content {
    display: contents;
  }

  .lp-media-accordion__heading {
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
    grid-row: 1;
    max-width: none;
  }

  /* Items and media keep the positions they had before the wrapper was unwrapped; with
     display:contents they need placing explicitly rather than by auto-flow. */
  .lp-media-accordion__items {
    grid-column: 1;
    grid-row: 2;
  }

  .lp-media-accordion__media {
    grid-column: 2;
    grid-row: 2;
  }

  /* Reference CTA row measured x=353 w=720, centred. An explicit width is needed rather than
     max-width alone: a grid item's auto width stretches to its area, so max-width clamps from
     above but never pulls it up to 720 from below. */
  .lp-media-accordion__cta-block {
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
    grid-row: 3;
    width: 72rem;
  }
}


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   12. The remainder, once the parent tracks stopped hiding it.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

/* The CTA row is still resolving flex/column from a media-gated rule. The earlier
   `display: block` sits outside any query, so the gated one wins on cascade order regardless
   of file position — it has to be restated inside a query of its own. Reference: block,
   align-items normal, measured 92px tall against our 78. */
@media screen and (min-width: 750px) {
  .lp-media-accordion__cta-block {
    display: block;
    align-items: normal;
  }
}

/* Now that the parent hands over exactly the reference's 1184px, the caps on these are
   redundant — and the reference itself declares none. Dropping them closes the declaration
   difference without changing a rendered pixel. */
.lp-science-tabs__tab-list,
.lp-science-tabs__panel,
.lp-science-tabs__panels {
  max-width: none;
}

/* Reference panel is a plain flex row with no gap; ours declared 0px, which computes
   differently even though it lays out the same. */
.lp-science-tabs__panel {
  column-gap: normal;
  row-gap: normal;
}

/* Reference tab bar measured 64px tall at 1440 and 86px at 390; ours 50 and 64. The
   difference is vertical padding on the bar itself, not on the tabs. */
.lp-science-tabs__tab-list {
  padding-top: 0.9rem;
  padding-bottom: 0.9rem;
}

/* Reference delivery block is a plain block with no gaps — the 12px grid gutters were ours.
   Its measured height of 346 against our 310 follows from that spacing being row margins
   rather than a grid gap. */
.lp-buy-box__delivery {
  display: block;
  column-gap: normal;
  row-gap: normal;
}

.lp-buy-box__delivery-card + .lp-buy-box__delivery-card {
  margin-top: 1.2rem;
}

@media screen and (max-width: 749px) {
  /* Reference content width at 390 is 343px across hero, accordion and buy box — ours ran 360
     because the page gutter is 15px where the reference uses 23.5px. */
  .lp-hero__container,
  .lp-media-accordion__inner {
    padding-left: 2.35rem;
    padding-right: 2.35rem;
  }

  /* The tab bar is the one element the reference lets run wider than the text column: measured
     359px starting at 16px, i.e. it breaks out of the 23.5px gutter to keep the scrollable row
     reaching the edge. */
  .lp-science-tabs__content {
    padding-left: 1.6rem;
    padding-right: 1.6rem;
  }
}


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   13. Specificity, because document order is not reliable here.

   The page loads lp-media-accordion.css TWICE — the second <link> sits after this section's
   <style>, so an equal-specificity rule here loses on order no matter that the file "comes
   last". Every rule below therefore carries the section prefix and wins at 0,3,0 rather than
   relying on where it happens to be parsed. This is why display:block on the CTA row kept
   resolving to flex/column across three rounds.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

.shopify-section[class*='lp-'] .lp-media-accordion__cta-block {
  display: block;
  align-items: normal;
  flex-direction: row;
}

/* Reference CTA row measured 92px tall at 1440 and 88 at 390 against our 78 — the button and
   the guarantee line sit further apart than ours did. */
.shopify-section[class*='lp-'] .lp-media-accordion__guarantee {
  margin-top: 1.2rem;
}

@media screen and (max-width: 749px) {
  /* Reference hero and accordion start at x=16 on mobile, not the 24 our gutter produced. */
  .lp-hero__container,
  .lp-media-accordion__inner {
    padding-left: 1.6rem;
    padding-right: 1.6rem;
  }

  /* With a 16px gutter the track is 358; the reference holds its text to 343, so the copy
     column carries the remainder as its own cap. */
  .lp-hero__content,
  .lp-media-accordion__heading,
  .lp-science-tabs__panel {
    max-width: 34.3rem;
  }
}


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   14. Last accountable pass.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

/* Reference content starts 7px further out than ours at every section: 129 against 136 on the
   accordion, 121 against 128 on the tabs, 353 against 360 on the CTA row. Same offset each
   time, so it is one gutter, not four bugs. */
@media screen and (min-width: 750px) {
  .lp-media-accordion__inner,
  .lp-science-tabs__content {
    padding-left: 0.9rem;
    padding-right: 0.9rem;
  }
}

/* The reference declares no cap on these — their width comes from the parent. Ours carried
   caps that now agree with the parent anyway, so removing them closes a declaration
   difference without moving anything. */
.lp-hero__heading {
  max-width: none;
}

@media screen and (max-width: 749px) {
  /* The 343px cap belongs on the elements the audit actually measures, not on their wrappers —
     capping .lp-hero__content left the h1 itself at the full 358. */
  .lp-hero__heading,
  .lp-media-accordion__cta-block {
    max-width: 34.3rem;
  }

  .lp-media-accordion__heading,
  .lp-science-tabs__panel {
    max-width: none;
  }

  /* Reference tab row starts at x=16; ours at 24. */
  .lp-science-tabs__content {
    padding-left: 1.6rem;
    padding-right: 1.6rem;
  }
}


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   15. What the independent visual check found that the property audit could not.

   A second model compared rendered screenshots against the reference and returned thirteen
   objections. Most of them sit on elements the pair list never named — which is the point: a
   measurement only ever finds what it was pointed at. These are its CSS-addressable findings.
   --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

@media screen and (min-width: 990px) {
  /* The columns were the wrong way round. The reference puts the nutrient wheel on the left
     and the accordion on the right; ours had it reversed. Nothing in the property audit
     compared these two containers to each other, so it read as no difference at all. */
  .lp-media-accordion__media {
    grid-column: 1;
    grid-row: 2;
  }

  .lp-media-accordion__items {
    grid-column: 2;
    grid-row: 2;
  }
}

/* Reference CTA spans nearly the full text column; ours sat at roughly half. */
.lp-hero__actions {
  align-items: stretch;
}

.lp-hero__section--product .lp-hero__cta {
  width: 100%;
}

/* Reference guarantee row is regular weight at a larger size with a leading icon and more
   room above it. Ours was smaller, bold, and tight against the button. */
.lp-hero__guarantee {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 0.8rem;
  margin-top: 1.6rem;
  font-size: 1.5rem;
  font-weight: 400;
}

/* Reference sales badge carries roomier horizontal padding. */
.lp-hero__stat-badge {
  padding-left: 2.4rem;
  padding-right: 2.4rem;
}

/* Reference testimonial: no divider, body copy upright rather than italic, attribution and
   rating on one bottom row. Ours italicised the quote and dropped the attribution below it. */
.lp-hero__testimonial blockquote,
.lp-hero__testimonial blockquote p {
  font-style: normal;
  border-left: 0;
  padding-left: 0;
}

.lp-hero__testimonial figcaption {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 0.8rem;
}

/* Reference hero media is a square 2x2 of equal tiles with sharp corners; ours rounded the
   frame and ran it as a wide rectangle. */
.lp-hero__media img,
.lp-hero__media picture {
  border-radius: 0;
}

.lp-hero__section--product .lp-hero__media {
  aspect-ratio: 1 / 1;
}

{% endstyle %}

